    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d456">

                <pb facs="0490" n="390" xml:id="pg390"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760c.61/222: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wallace</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-08-20T18:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 20,
                                1920—6 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 8:58 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1582. Have carried out instructions given in Department’s 1374, August 13, 6 p.m.
                    I was assured by Paleologue<anchor n="16" xml:id="d456-f1"/> that France had not
                    the least intention of sending supplies or troops to Poland across German
                    territory. Goeppert,<anchor n="17" xml:id="d456-f2"/> who left recently to go to
                    Berlin, was so informed by Paléologue.</p>
                <p>Paléologue repeated that if Germany tried to nullify the provisions of the peace
                    treaty in the east, France would take action on the Rhine. This attitude had
                    been reported already to the Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d456-f1" n="16" xml:id="d456-f1-content">
                <p>Maurice G. Paléologue, secretary general of the French Ministry of Foreign
                    Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d456-f2" n="17" xml:id="d456-f2-content">
                <p>Heinrich Goeppert, president of the German delegation to the Peace
                    Conference.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
